            0.145,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "96"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.477,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari |Grade 9| Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It is natural to ask: How many circles can you draw through three distinct points A, B and C on a plane? Is there always at least one such circle? Not necessarily! What if A, B and C lie on a straight line, i.e., are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "collinear",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Can you explain why, in this case, there is no circle through A, B and C?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.204,
            0.886,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us assume that A, B and C are not collinear. Is there always a circle passing through A, B and C? Can there be more than one circle through A, B and C?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.268,
            0.885,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " There is a unique circle passing through three non-collinear points."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.311,
            0.886,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is this true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If there is a circle that passes through non-collinear A, B and C, the circle must have a centre. Let’s call that centre O (we will discover O soon)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.37,
            0.883,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since OA = OB, we know that O lies on the perpendicular bisector of AB."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.39,
            0.886,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since OA = OC, we know that O lies on the perpendicular bisector of AC as well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.427,
            0.886,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "But A, B and C are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "not",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " collinear. So, the perpendicular bisectors of AB and AC will intersect at a unique point, since two intersecting lines meet at exactly one point on the plane. That point must be O."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.486,
            0.885,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using O as the centre we can draw a circle with radius equal to the length of OA. That will pass through A, B and C. This explains why the statement is true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.549,
            0.885,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now three non-collinear points also determine a triangle. What we have described above is the construction of a circle that passes through the vertices of the triangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.618,
            0.652,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perpendicular bisector of BC"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.652,
            0.405,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perpendicular bisector of AB"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
